# vendor_init needs write access to the vendor_internal_prop types this device
# declares in property.te. Without these, every one of those properties is
# silently dropped the moment SELinux goes enforcing.
#
# Why it is needed at all: property.te turns a set of prefixes that used to fall
# through to vendor_default_prop into dedicated vendor_internal_prop types. That
# is the right call for privilege separation, but vendor_init's default write
# access covers vendor_default_prop ONLY, so labelling a property that
# /vendor/build.prop assigns takes away the permission that made it work.
#
# ⚠️ property.te:8-13 justifies the labelling by claiming vendor_default_prop
# cannot be set by vendor_init. That is INVERTED. The neverallow at
# system/sepolicy/private/domain.te:841 explicitly excludes vendor_init, and
# private/vendor_init.te:312 grants it. Do not reason from that comment.
#
# Measured, not assumed. Both of these appear in work/first-boot/s12-boot1:
#
#   avc: denied { set } property=vendor.thermal.mode
#        scontext=u:r:vendor_init:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:vendor_thermal_prop:s0
#   avc: denied { set } property=persist.vendor.lgsi.adbdebug
#        scontext=u:r:vendor_init:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:vendor_lenovotb_init_prop:s0
#
# They do not break anything today only because this is a permissive bring-up
# build -- CheckMacPerms (system/core/init/property_service.cpp:163-177) returns
# success when permissive, so the property is stored anyway.
#
# What each one costs under enforcing:
#
#   vendor_thermal_prop        vendor.thermal.mode is what gates the whole
#                              on property:vendor.thermal.mode=* block in
#                              init.qcom.rc:524. Lose it and thermal-switch-engine
#                              never starts and none of the seven
#                              thermal-engine-malbec-*.conf policies load. That is
#                              exactly the failure session 11 added the property to
#                              fix, so losing it silently undoes that work.
#
#   vendor_lenovotb_init_prop  init.target.rc:270 does
#                              `setprop persist.vendor.lgsi.adbdebug
#                               ${ro.boot.lgsi.adbdebug}` from a vendor rc, and the
#                              same denial covers vendor_init READING the
#                              ${ro.odm.*} values it expands elsewhere in that file.
#
# Pattern and precedent: device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr/sm8750/generic/vendor/common/
# vendor_init.te:75-109 does exactly this for QTI's own vendor_internal_prop types.
set_prop(vendor_init, vendor_thermal_prop)
set_prop(vendor_init, vendor_lenovotb_init_prop)

# rootdir/etc/init.malbec.rc does `write /proc/support_pen 1` from an
# `on property:sys.boot_completed=1` block to re-enable active-stylus scanning
# in the touch controller (the driver disables it at boot -- see that file).
#
# Commands in a /vendor/etc/init/*.rc run in the vendor_init subcontext
# (system/core/init/subcontext.cpp), so this is the domain that needs the write,
# not init. The node is labelled proc_lenovo_pen by our own genfs_contexts:11-43.
#
# open is required as well as write: the `write` builtin is open(O_WRONLY|O_CREAT
# |O_TRUNC) followed by write(), so a write-only allow rule denies at open().
#
# No neverallow stands in the way -- the constraints on vendor_init and proc_type
# are about /proc/sysrq-trigger and the sysfs/proc *_type attributes used for
# system-owned tunables; proc_lenovo_pen is a device-tree-local type over a
# vendor driver's own procfs node, which is exactly the case
# device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr covers the same way for QTI's touch nodes.
allow vendor_init proc_lenovo_pen:file { open write getattr };

# ⚠️ NOT granted, deliberately: vendor_init -> build_prop / build_bootimage_prop.
#
# Two more property-set denials show up on every boot:
#
#   avc: denied { set } property=ro.system.build.fingerprint     tcontext=build_prop
#   avc: denied { set } property=ro.bootimage.build.fingerprint  tcontext=build_bootimage_prop
#
# The source is the factory init.target.rc:278-283, `on property:
# ro.odm.fingerprint.update=*`, which rewrites the fingerprint across vendor /
# system / product / odm / bootimage. That is Lenovo's per-unit fingerprint
# mechanism and it must NOT be granted here:
#
#   1. It cannot succeed anyway. Those are ro.* properties, which are write-once,
#      and build.prop has already set them by the time this trigger fires.
#   2. If it ever did succeed it would overwrite the fingerprint PixelOS manages
#      (SetSafetyNetProps(), system/core/init/property_service.cpp:1377-1389).
#
# The denial is expected noise, not a defect. Leave it.

# Charging control gate. init.malbec.rc opens /sys/class/qcom-battery/
# charge_control_en at boot and parks the two threshold nodes at "no limit";
# the derivation is in the rc file. Without this, Settings' Charging control
# would appear and silently do nothing under enforcing.
allow vendor_init sysfs_qcom_battery:dir search;
allow vendor_init sysfs_qcom_battery:file rw_file_perms;
allow vendor_init vendor_sysfs_battery_supply:dir search;
allow vendor_init vendor_sysfs_battery_supply:file rw_file_perms;
# ★ setattr, for the `chown system system input_suspend` in the same rc block.
# rw_file_perms above does NOT include setattr, and without it the chown that
# makes charging control work at all is denied the day SELinux goes enforcing —
# which would silently put the feature straight back into the state the rc file
# documents at length (HAL runs as uid 1000, node is 0664 root:root, every write
# returns EACCES).
#
# Stock has no equivalent grant, and that is not an argument for leaving it out:
# stock's own `chmod 0666 input_suspend` sits in init.target.rc's `on boot`,
# where the node does not exist yet, so its denial has never had a chance to be
# logged. Ours runs where the node is provably present.
allow vendor_init vendor_sysfs_battery_supply:file setattr;

# Touch mode. rootdir/etc/init.malbec.rc writes /proc/HighReportRate from
# `on property:persist.sys.malbec.touch_mode=*`, which MalbecParts sets when the
# owner picks stylus or game mode.
#
# Same reasoning as the proc_lenovo_pen grant above, and the same domain: the rc
# file is under /vendor/etc/init, so its commands run in the vendor_init
# subcontext (system/core/init/subcontext.cpp), not in init. `open` is required
# as well as `write` because init's `write` builtin is
# open(O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC) followed by write(), so a write-only rule denies
# at open().
#
# The node is labelled proc_lenovo_touch by our own genfs_contexts, and the
# Lenovo touchscreen HAL already holds rw_file_perms on that type
# (hal_lenovo.te). This is the second writer, not a new privilege class.
#
# ⚠️ Why the write is here and not in the app: proc_lenovo_touch is a vendor
# type. Granting system_app write access to it would be a coredomain reaching
# into vendor storage — exactly the boundary the rest of this policy avoids —
# and it would have to be added to BOARD_PLAT_PRIVATE_SEPOLICY_DIR, which this
# device does not otherwise need. Routing through a property that system_app can
# already set costs one trigger block and no new policy at all.
allow vendor_init proc_lenovo_touch:file { open write getattr };
